<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adad-nirari II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 911–891 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adad-nirari II was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from 911 to 891 BCE and is counted as the first king of the Neo-Assyrian period. He campaigned against the Aramaean states of upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, collecting tribute from rulers along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a>, and fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, recovering territory and fixing a border by treaty. His wars began the long recovery of Assyrian power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-ii.jpg" alt="Adad-nirari II" width="320" height="259" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inscribed_stone_tablet_of_Adad-nirari_II_from_Assur,_912-891_BCE._Iraq_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adad-nirari II's name means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>, the storm god, is my help. His father was Ashur-dan II, whom he succeeded after a minor dynastic struggle. The accession probably encouraged revolts among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>'s nominal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> in nearby regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. He began the first renewed period of major expansion after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-assyrian-empire">Middle Assyrian Empire</a>, which had begun in 1365 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-uballit-i">Ashur-uballit I</a> and had ended after the death of Ashur-bel-kala in 1053 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Adad-nirari II firmly subjugated areas that had been only nominally under Assyrian vassalage. After a battle at the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> rivers in 910 BCE he conquered and deported troublesome Arameans. He then subdued Neo-Hittite and Hurrian populations in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. In the same year he twice attacked and defeated Shamash-mudammiq of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, annexing a large area north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river">Diyala River</a> and the towns of Hit and Zanqu in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, and later in his reign he made further gains against Nabu-shuma-ukin I.</p><p>To the west he subjugated the Aramean cities of Kadmuh and Nisibin and their territories, collected vast amounts of treasure and secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> river region. His reign was a time of returning prosperity in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>, with the expansion of Phoenician and Aramaean trade routes linking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His son Tukulti-Ninurta II continued to expand Assyrian territory and wage war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a>'s enemies. Full eponym lists survive from Adad-nirari's reign down to the middle of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a> in the 7th century BCE, so year one of his reign in 911 BCE is perhaps the first event in ancient Near Eastern history that can be dated to an exact year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adad-nirari II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's greatest phase was the Neo-Assyrian revival, which began in 911 BCE under <b>Adad-nirari II</b> and culminated in Esarhaddon's conquest of Egypt in 671 BCE, when Assyria became the largest empire the world had yet seen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 894 BCE its king Abi-salamu paid tribute to <b>Adad-nirari II</b> of the Assyrian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/bit-bahiani">Bit Bahiani</a></li></ul></section>
